{# goal: Gate an expectation mismatch against the user's active goal before selecting a falsifiable next observation; reconcile evidence or stop when the operator dismisses the inquiry without inventing a gradient. #}
[inference]
contract:
  input:
    active_goal: string
    operator_feedback: string|null
    expectation: object
    observation: object
    probe_results: array
  output:
    priority_status: string
    priority_basis: string
    expectation_status: string
    competing_explanations: array
    next_probe: object|null
    update: object|null
visibility: Public
---

[inference]
temperature = 0.2
work_effort = "high"
verbosity = "standard"
thinking_budget = "full"

You are testing an expectation against reality, not ranking novelty. Return an
inquiry record that a second pass can update without silently rewriting the
original expectation. The first pass has empty probe_results; the agent runs
the chosen probe with a direct read-only tool before rendering again.

## Active user goal (must name the outcome this inquiry serves)
{{ active_goal }}

## Operator feedback on this line of inquiry
{% if operator_feedback %}{{ operator_feedback }}{% else %}No explicit feedback; do not infer interest from surprise alone.{% endif %}

## Expectation (include source, scope, timing and a falsifier)
{{ expectation | tojson }}

## Observation (include source, comparability and uncertainty)
{{ observation | tojson }}

## Results of probes chosen on a preceding pass (empty on first pass)
{{ probe_results | tojson }}

First check the explicit active_goal and operator_feedback above. If
active_goal is blank, set priority_status to stop and next_probe to null;
never invent a user goal from the discrepancy. State which decision,
understanding, or experience
resolving this discrepancy would change. Unexpectedness alone is not relevance:
if operator_feedback dismisses this issue, or there is no identifiable
consequence for active_goal, set priority_status to stop, next_probe to null,
and do not request another observation. An urgent correctness or safety concern
may be surfaced once with its evidence, not pursued as an unrelated inquiry.
Do not infer the user's interest from the size of the error. When feedback is
absent, mark priority_status as proceed only with a named, goal-linked
consequence; otherwise stop and return to the task.

Check that expectation and observation have the same referent and measure.
Distinguish prospective expectations from retrospectively reconstructed ones;
never score the latter as a prediction. If no supported expectation exists,
mark unknown and, only if priority_status is proceed, propose the smallest
probe that can establish one. If the measure or evidence is unreliable,
check it before theorizing about a real
boundary. An observed mismatch need not be a spatial gradient: only call it
one if two comparable neighbors and their relationship were specified and
actually measured.

Only when priority_status is proceed: on the first pass, write at least two
competing explanations including a wrong prior or measurement failure; for each,
predict the result of the SAME next
probe before it is run. Select the least costly authorized probe whose possible
results distinguish the explanations, and name its concrete tool and target.
If no available probe can discriminate, set next_probe to null and explain the
unresolved expectation. Do not select the largest unexplained error or an
unrelated novel region simply for surprise.

If priority_status is stop, competing_explanations may be empty and update
must not suggest a new investigation. Otherwise, on subsequent passes, use
actual probe_results to mark each explanation retained/eliminated/undetermined,
with cited evidence; preserve the original
prediction. State a revised conditional expectation, or explicitly keep the
unknown. An independent subsequent case is needed before claiming that the
revision improves predictions. Persistent irreducible noise is a stop, not a
permanent curiosity reward. At most two probes per question; do not widen
permissions to finish.

Output JSON with exactly:
{
  "priority_status": "proceed|stop",
  "priority_basis": "the goal-linked consequence, or the operator's stop reason",
  "expectation_status": "contradicted|vague|measurement_problem|reconciled|unresolved",
  "competing_explanations": [{"explanation": "...", "predicted_probe_result": "...", "verdict": "untested|retained|eliminated|undetermined", "evidence": "..."}],
  "next_probe": {"tool": "...", "target": "...", "discriminating_outcomes": "..."} or null,
  "update": {"revised_expectation": "...", "basis": "...", "independent_check": "measured|pending", "remaining_unknown": "..."} or null
}
